1
00:00:00,274 --> 00:00:03,362
For centuries, the world
called this country Persia.

2
00:00:03,908 --> 00:00:09,597
Then, in 1935, its king asked
everyone to use a new name: Iran.

3
00:00:10,135 --> 00:00:12,404
He picked one date for the change.

4
00:00:12,424 --> 00:00:14,713
March 21. The first day of spring.

5
00:00:15,248 --> 00:00:16,998
I will tell you why at the end.

6
00:00:17,018 --> 00:00:20,215
First, let me show you a
country you will want to visit.

7
00:00:20,734 --> 00:00:24,657
This is Iran. About 92
million people live here.

8
00:00:24,677 --> 00:00:26,484
The capital is Tehran.

9
00:00:27,056 --> 00:00:31,266
The language is Persian, and you
say hello with one word: salaam.

10
00:00:31,286 --> 00:00:33,954
Dozens of other languages are spoken too.

11
00:00:34,446 --> 00:00:39,230
Prices are spoken in tomans, but
the real currency is the rial.

12
00:00:39,250 --> 00:00:41,233
One toman equals 10 rials.

13
00:00:41,726 --> 00:00:44,752
Now, four facts that will surprise you.

14
00:00:44,773 --> 00:00:49,850
Fact 1. Nearly 90 percent of the
world's saffron comes from Iran.

15
00:00:50,555 --> 00:00:56,059
Fact 2. In winter, you can ride a
gondola from Tehran up to a ski resort,

16
00:00:56,079 --> 00:00:58,274
more than 3, 700 meters high.

17
00:00:59,051 --> 00:01:04,506
Fact 3. Not far away, Mount Damavand
is the highest volcano in Asia.

18
00:01:05,212 --> 00:01:06,961
Fact 4. In the Lut Desert,

19
00:01:06,981 --> 00:01:10,840
satellites have measured sand
at over 70 degrees Celsius.

20
00:01:10,860 --> 00:01:14,670
That is one of the hottest
places ever recorded on land.

21
00:01:15,246 --> 00:01:19,976
And yet, people here made ice in
the desert, over 2, 000 years ago.

22
00:01:19,996 --> 00:01:23,167
They built giant clay
cones called yakhchals,

23
00:01:23,187 --> 00:01:25,385
and kept ice frozen all summer.

24
00:01:26,238 --> 00:01:29,391
Now, where would you go? Start in Isfahan.

25
00:01:29,411 --> 00:01:32,886
An old Persian saying
calls it half the world.

26
00:01:33,514 --> 00:01:38,148
Its great square, Naqsh-e Jahan,
is more than 500 meters long,

27
00:01:38,168 --> 00:01:41,621
and it is one of the
largest squares on Earth.

28
00:01:42,314 --> 00:01:46,243
Next, go south, to Shiraz.
In the Nasir al-Mulk Mosque,

29
00:01:46,263 --> 00:01:49,833
called the Pink Mosque,
come early in the morning.

30
00:01:49,853 --> 00:01:54,807
The sun shines through stained glass
and paints colors on the carpet.

31
00:01:55,603 --> 00:02:01,754
A short drive away stands Persepolis,
founded by Darius the Great in 518 B.

32
00:02:01,774 --> 00:02:04,881
C.. By Guinness's count, around 480 B.

33
00:02:04,901 --> 00:02:09,427
C. this empire ruled 44
percent of all people on Earth.

34
00:02:10,511 --> 00:02:13,862
Then Yazd, a city of
mud brick in the desert.

35
00:02:13,882 --> 00:02:17,552
Tall towers catch the
breeze and cool the houses.

36
00:02:18,511 --> 00:02:24,846
Now, let us eat. The national dish is
chelow kabab: grilled meat, with rice.

37
00:02:25,418 --> 00:02:27,661
But ask any Iranian about tahdig.

38
00:02:27,681 --> 00:02:30,198
The word means the bottom of the pot.

39
00:02:30,218 --> 00:02:34,950
It is the golden, crunchy crust of
rice, and everybody wants a piece.

40
00:02:35,660 --> 00:02:39,930
For dessert, try faloodeh, a
frozen dessert of thin noodles.

41
00:02:39,950 --> 00:02:44,312
Yes, noodles. It was made with
ice from those old ice houses.

42
00:02:45,250 --> 00:02:50,153
Then there are the carpets. Persian
weavers dye the wool with plants,

43
00:02:50,173 --> 00:02:54,576
like madder and indigo, even
walnut skin and pomegranate skin.

44
00:02:54,596 --> 00:02:57,807
UNESCO listed Persian
carpet weaving in 2010.

45
00:02:58,763 --> 00:03:03,750
Poetry matters here too. In Shiraz,
people visit the tomb of the poet Hafez.

46
00:03:03,770 --> 00:03:07,373
Many Iranians ask his book
a question about the future.

47
00:03:07,393 --> 00:03:10,950
They open it at random, and
the page gives the answer.

48
00:03:11,940 --> 00:03:14,636
But the best time to visit is spring.

49
00:03:14,656 --> 00:03:20,067
Nowruz means new day. It begins at the
exact moment of the spring equinox,

50
00:03:20,087 --> 00:03:22,803
and it is more than 3, 000 years old.

51
00:03:23,380 --> 00:03:25,693
First, families clean the whole house.

52
00:03:25,713 --> 00:03:30,052
On the last Tuesday night of the
year, people jump over small bonfires,

53
00:03:30,072 --> 00:03:32,528
to leave the old year's bad luck behind.

54
00:03:33,458 --> 00:03:36,106
Then comes the table. The haft-seen:

55
00:03:36,126 --> 00:03:40,627
seven things whose names all
start with the Persian letter S.

56
00:03:40,647 --> 00:03:44,056
Sprouts, an apple, garlic,
vinegar, and sumac.

57
00:03:44,732 --> 00:03:47,381
The new year lasts 13 days. On the 13th,

58
00:03:47,401 --> 00:03:50,804
almost the whole country
goes outside for a picnic.

59
00:03:51,252 --> 00:03:54,236
Now you can see why the
king chose March 21.

60
00:03:54,256 --> 00:03:58,216
Nowruz, the first day of spring,
is Iran's oldest holiday.

61
00:03:58,671 --> 00:04:03,086
And the name Iran was not new.
Iranians had used it for centuries,

62
00:04:03,106 --> 00:04:05,572
while the world said Persia. In 1935,

63
00:04:05,592 --> 00:04:08,683
the king simply asked
the world to use it too.

64
00:04:09,386 --> 00:04:11,597
Did the world obey? Not entirely.

65
00:04:11,617 --> 00:04:14,504
In 1959, his son said
both names were fine.

66
00:04:14,524 --> 00:04:18,242
We still say Persian carpets,
and the Persian language.

67
00:04:20,691 --> 00:04:23,353
That was Iran. Which country
should we visit next?

68
00:04:23,373 --> 00:04:24,661
Tell me in the comments.

